A calendar identifier is the identifier, not the name of the symbol.

Every NSCalendarIdentifier constant held its own spelling: NSCalendarIdentifierGregorian was the
string "NSCalendarIdentifierGregorian", not "gregorian". -[NSCalendar initWithCalendarIdentifier:]
hands that to CFCalendarCreateWithIdentifier, which compares against the real CLDR identifiers and
returns NULL for anything else, so the documented call returned nil. An application then read year,
month and day FROM NIL and got ZERO for each, with no error, no exception and no log line. All
sixteen were wrong.

And +calendarWithIdentifier: did not exist, neither declared nor implemented, although it has been
the documented way to get a calendar since 10.9. Calling it took doesNotRecognizeSelector: and an
uncaught exception, which exits 0.

MEASURED with tests/foundation/probe_date_formatting.m, before and after:
    before   13 checks, 4 mismatched: year 0, month 0, day 0, and the control day 0
    after    18 checks, 0 mismatched: 2001, 9, 9, and the control 10
The probe separates NIL from ZERO on purpose, because a message to nil answers 0 and the two need
different fixes. That is what turned "the calendar is wrong" into "the calendar is nil", and
printing the constant next to the literal @"gregorian" named the rest in one run.

ALREADY CORRECT AND RE-CONFIRMED: NSDateFormatter formats, parses and round trips; NSNumberFormatter
handles decimals, fraction digits and negatives. The kCF constants hold the right CLDR values
already; only the NS wrappers were placeholders.

A LIMITATION LEFT IN PLACE AND NAMED RATHER THAN PAPERED OVER: CFCalendarCreateWithIdentifier
accepts only gregorian, buddhist, japanese, islamic, islamic-civil and hebrew. The other ten
constants are now CORRECT constants for a calendar this port cannot construct.

Task #253.

